  "notes": "HOW ke0 IS DERIVED FROM tPeak (R/getDrugPK.R).  For an IV drug (tPeakRoute 'IV', no X$ke0 supplied) getDrugPK() runs   ke0 <- stats::optimize(tPeakError, c(0, 200), tPeak, p_coef_bolus_l1, p_coef_bolus_l2, p_coef_bolus_l3, lambda_1, lambda_2, lambda_3)$minimum i.e. Brent's fmin on the interval [0, 200] /min with the DEFAULT tolerance tol = .Machine$double.eps^0.25 = 2^-13 = 0.0001220703125 (absolute, in ke0 units).  The objective tPeakError(lambda_4 = ke0, tPeak, p1, p2, p3, l1, l2, l3) is:   e_i = p_i * ke0 / (ke0 - l_i) for the three plasma exponentials (e_2, e_3 are 0 when l_2, l_3 are 0), e_4 = -(e_1 + e_2 + e_3);   predPeak = stats::optimize(CE, c(0, 100), e_1, e_2, e_3, e_4, l_1, l_2, l_3, ke0, maximum = TRUE)$maximum, where   CE(t) = e_1 exp(-l_1 t) + e_2 exp(-l_2 t) + e_3 exp(-l_3 t) + e_4 exp(-ke0 t) is the effect-site concentration after a   UNIT IV BOLUS (the plasma curve is the bolus response sum p_i exp(-l_i t), p_i = p_coef_bolus_li);   return (tPeak - predPeak)^2. So it is a NESTED Brent minimisation: the inner optimize (maximum = TRUE, interval [0, 100] min, same default tol) finds the peak time of the unit-bolus effect-site curve for a trial ke0; the outer one minimises the squared miss of tPeak. stats::optimize(maximum = TRUE) minimises function(arg) -f(arg, ...) and returns list(maximum = argmin, objective = f(argmin)). The C routine is Brent_fmin() in R's src/library/stats/src/optimize.c (Netlib FMIN, Forsythe-Malcolm-Moler):   c = (3 - sqrt(5))/2; eps = sqrt(DBL_EPSILON); x = v = w = a + c (b - a); fx = f(x); d = e = 0; tol3 = tol/3;   loop: xm = (a+b)/2; tol1 = eps |x| + tol3; t2 = 2 tol1; stop when |x - xm| <= t2 - (b - a)/2;   if |e| > tol1 fit a parabola through (v,fv),(w,fw),(x,fx): r = (x-w)(fx-fv); q = (x-v)(fx-fw); p = (x-v) q - (x-w) r;   q = 2 (q - r); if q > 0 then p = -p else q = -q; r = e; e = d;   golden-section step (e = (x < xm ? b : a) - x; d = c e) when |p| >= |q r / 2| or p <= q (a - x) or p >= q (b - x),   else parabolic step d = p/q, u = x + d, and if u - a < t2 or b - u < t2 then d = (x >= xm ? -tol1 : tol1);   u = x + d if |d| >= tol1 else x +/- tol1 (sign of d); fu = f(u);   if fu <= fx: (u < x ? b = x : a = x); v = w; w = x; x = u; fv = fw; fw = fx; fx = fu;   else: (u < x ? a = u : b = u); if fu <= fw or w == x: v = w, fv = fw, w = u, fw = fu; else if fu <= fv or v == x or v == w: v = u, fv = fu.   return x. The pure-R transcription brentFmin() in generate.R, applied to BOTH levels, reproduced every ke0 in this file bit for bit (see pureRBrentIdentical), so a JavaScript port of the same algorithm with the same tolerance should match R's ke0 to within a few ulps whenever its exp() agrees with R's libm; a last-bit difference in exp() can in principle change a branch and move the result by up to the stopping width (about 2 tol1 = 2 (sqrt(eps) |ke0| + tol/3), i.e. ~8e-5 absolute). ACHIEVABLE ACCURACY WITHOUT PORTING BRENT: because the outer tolerance is absolute 1.22e-4 and the inner peak time is itself only resolved to ~4e-5 min, R's ke0 is NOT the exact root.  `ke0Exact` is the ke0 whose exact (uniroot, tol 1e-14) peak time equals tPeak; `ke0ExactRelativeDifference` shows how far R's value is from it (of order 1e-5 to 1e-4 relative). A port using a different (better) optimizer should therefore expect agreement with R's ke0 only to that order; to validate coefficients and controller output independently of the solve, inject R's ke0 from models.json. ROUND TRIP: `roundTrip.peakTimeInnerOptimize` is the inner optimizer's peak time for R's ke0 (what the solve 'saw'); `roundTrip.peakTimeExact` is the true peak time of the unit-bolus Ce curve for that ke0; `ceAtPeak*` is Ce there (its reciprocal is getDrugPK()'s vdPeakEffect).  `objectiveSamples` give tPeakError() and the inner predPeak at ke0 x {0.5, 0.8, 0.95, 1, 1.05, 1.25, 2} so a port can check its objective function (including the inner optimizer) directly. Both drugs: tPeak = 1.6 min (propofol: Schnider 1999; remifentanil: 'Opioid simulation spreadsheet'), tPeakRoute IV.",
